//! crypto/secp256k1_ecdsa.rye — Season G (Cryptography): ECDSA signature VERIFICATION on secp256k1 authored in pure Rye, composed entirely
//! over the GREEN rungs beneath it — the base field (crypto/fe_secp256k1.rye), the group law (crypto/secp256k1_group.rye), the scalar ladder
//! (crypto/secp256k1_scalarmul.rye), and the scalar field modulo the group order n (crypto/secp256k1_scalar.rye). It authors no new
//! cryptography; it is the assembly the whole secp256k1 tower was climbing toward — the signature scheme Bitcoin and Ethereum sign with —
//! and the exact sibling of crypto/ed25519_verify.rye over the edwards25519 group. Verification is the non-gated half of ECDSA: every value
//! it touches is PUBLIC, so it holds no key and signs nothing.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season G thread (Cryptography · Rye-native, parity-checked, audit-ready).
//! Design read: active-designing/date/20260816/20260816-025516_secp256k1-ecdsa-verify.md (this rung's claim) and
//! active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-175524_rye-first-crypto-parity-and-the-decision-wave.md (the priority spine: Rye first, Glow on green Rye).
//! Kin: crypto/secp256k1_scalar.rye (the order field ECDSA runs in, GREEN) · crypto/secp256k1_scalarmul.rye (the point ladder, GREEN) ·
//! crypto/secp256k1_group.rye (the group law, GREEN) · crypto/fe_secp256k1.rye (the base field, GREEN) · crypto/ed25519_verify.rye (the
//! sibling verifier over the twisted Edwards group). secp256k1 is not in Monocypher, so its source parity is not a rung here; the algebraic
//! known-answers plus Zig's independent std.crypto.sign.ecdsa.EcdsaSecp256k1Sha256 are the two witnesses (.claude/rules/gratitude-licenses.md).
//!
//! THE CRUX — SEC 1 verification, exactly. Given a public key Q = (Qx, Qy), a message hash z, and a signature (r, s): reject unless r and s
//! both lie in [1, n−1]; fold the hash to a scalar z mod n; compute w = s⁻¹ mod n, then the two scalars u1 = z·w mod n and u2 = r·w mod n;
//! form the point R = u1·G + u2·Q over the GREEN ladder and complete group law; reject if R is the point at infinity; and accept exactly when
//! the affine x-coordinate of R, reduced mod n, equals r. The base point G is the SEC 2 generator, entered as its published big-endian
//! coordinate bytes and parsed through the proven base field (never raw limbs), with Z = 1. One honest strengthening beyond the reference's
//! verify path: because this file takes affine coordinates directly rather than a validated PublicKey, it adds an ON-CURVE check (y² = x³ + 7
//! in the base field) and refuses a public key that does not satisfy the curve equation. The one product with the outside world is byte order
//! — the ecosystem encodes r, s, z, and coordinates big-endian while the scalar field and the ladder walk little-endian, so this file reverses
//! at its boundary and keeps the arithmetic in its native order.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE — software only, purely LOCAL, no network, no funds, no real device, no identity. In ECDSA VERIFICATION every value — r, s, z,
//! Q, and the inverse — is PUBLIC, so the variable-time reduction, inversion, and double-and-add ladder leak no secret; that is precisely why
//! verification is the non-gated rung. This file holds no key and calls no std.crypto in the primitive. ECDSA SIGNING with a real private key
//! (Q = d·G for a secret d, and a per-message nonce k) stays the custody gate (#3/#4) — the agent builds and proves the verifier; the key
//! stays the maintainer's hand — and a constant-time signing arithmetic stays the Season's named horizon. Parity is proven at selftest against
//! two references runnable now — algebraic known-answers anyone can check (a valid signature verifies; a one-bit tamper of r, s, z, or a
//! coordinate is refused; an all-zero r or s is refused; an off-curve key is refused; and (r, n−s) verifies exactly when (r, s) does), and
//! Zig's own std.crypto.sign.ecdsa.EcdsaSecp256k1Sha256 as an independent implementation, true-for-true and false-for-false across a spread of
//! deterministic key pairs and messages.
//!
//! Run from the repository root:
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run crypto/secp256k1_ecdsa.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;
const fe = @import("fe_secp256k1.rye");
const pt = @import("secp256k1_group.rye");
const mul_pt = @import("secp256k1_scalarmul.rye");
const sc = @import("secp256k1_scalar.rye");

pub const secp256k1_ecdsa_version = "20260816.025516";

/// The one fixed width this rung works in: a 32-byte coordinate, scalar, or hash — the width every secp256k1 value encodes. Named so the array
/// bounds are a stated constant rather than a literal buried in the walk.
pub const field_bytes: u32 = 32;

/// The x-coordinate of the SEC 2 base point G, big-endian — the published generator of secp256k1's prime-order group. A defining curve
/// parameter (like b = 7), entered as bytes and parsed through the proven base field, never as raw limbs.
const g_x_be = [32]u8{
    0x79, 0xbe, 0x66, 0x7e, 0xf9, 0xdc, 0xbb, 0xac, 0x55, 0xa0, 0x62, 0x95, 0xce, 0x87, 0x0b, 0x07,
    0x02, 0x9b, 0xfc, 0xdb, 0x2d, 0xce, 0x28, 0xd9, 0x59, 0xf2, 0x81, 0x5b, 0x16, 0xf8, 0x17, 0x98,
};

/// The y-coordinate of the SEC 2 base point G, big-endian — the second published generator coordinate, parsed through the proven base field.
const g_y_be = [32]u8{
    0x48, 0x3a, 0xda, 0x77, 0x26, 0xa3, 0xc4, 0x65, 0x5d, 0xa4, 0xfb, 0xfc, 0x0e, 0x11, 0x08, 0xa8,
    0xfd, 0x17, 0xb4, 0x48, 0xa6, 0x85, 0x54, 0x19, 0x9c, 0x47, 0xd0, 0x8f, 0xfb, 0x10, 0xd4, 0xb8,
};

/// The field element 1 — the projective Z of an affine point (x, y) lifted to (X : Y : 1).
fn fe_one() fe.Field {
    return fe.Field{ .l = .{ 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0 } };
}

/// The base point G in projective coordinates (Gx : Gy : 1), built from its published big-endian coordinate bytes through the proven base
/// field. Callers of scalarmul never see infinity here — G is a finite generator, so Z = 1 ≠ 0.
fn base_point() pt.Point {
    const g = pt.Point{ .x = fe.from_bytes(&g_x_be), .y = fe.from_bytes(&g_y_be), .z = fe_one() };
    // invariant: the base point is finite — its projective Z is exactly 1, never the point at infinity.
    assert(g.z.l[0] == 1 and g.z.l[7] == 0);
    return g;
}

/// Reverse a 32-byte encoding between big-endian (the ecosystem convention for r, s, z, and coordinates) and little-endian (the order the
/// scalar field and the ladder walk). The boundary courtesy this file keeps so the arithmetic stays in its native order.
fn reverse32(s: *const [32]u8) [32]u8 {
    var r: [32]u8 = undefined;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < field_bytes) : (i += 1) {
        // invariant: source and mirror index both stay inside the 32-byte width.
        assert(i < field_bytes and (field_bytes - 1 - i) < field_bytes);
        r[i] = s[field_bytes - 1 - i];
    }
    return r;
}

/// True when a 32-byte encoding is all zero — the below-1 half of the [1, n−1] range gate, and the point-at-infinity test on a packed Z.
fn is_zero32(s: *const [32]u8) bool {
    var acc: u8 = 0;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < field_bytes) : (i += 1) acc |= s[i];
    return acc == 0;
}

/// True when two 32-byte encodings are equal, compared without an early return so the compare is data-independent.
fn eq32(a: *const [32]u8, b: *const [32]u8) bool {
    var diff: u8 = 0;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < field_bytes) : (i += 1) diff |= a[i] ^ b[i];
    return diff == 0;
}

/// Fold a big-endian 32-byte value modulo the group order n into a canonical little-endian scalar — the hash-to-scalar step, the same
/// reduction the reference runs. The big-endian value is reversed into the low 32 bytes of a 64-byte little-endian buffer, then reduced.
fn hash_to_scalar(be: *const [32]u8) [32]u8 {
    const le = reverse32(be);
    var wide = [_]u8{0} ** 64;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < field_bytes) : (i += 1) {
        // invariant: the reversed value lands entirely in the low half of the wide buffer.
        assert(i < 64);
        wide[i] = le[i];
    }
    return sc.reduce(&wide);
}

/// True when the affine point (Qx, Qy) satisfies the curve equation y² = x³ + 7 in the base field — the on-curve gate this file adds beyond
/// the reference's verify path, so an off-curve public key is refused rather than trusted. Both coordinates arrive big-endian.
pub fn on_curve(qx_be: *const [32]u8, qy_be: *const [32]u8) bool {
    const x = fe.from_bytes(qx_be);
    const y = fe.from_bytes(qy_be);
    const lhs = fe.square(y); // y²
    const x3 = fe.mul(fe.square(x), x); // x³
    const b = fe.Field{ .l = .{ 7, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0 } }; // the curve constant b = 7
    const rhs = fe.add(x3, b); // x³ + 7
    // invariant: the comparison is over canonical packed bytes, so equal field values always compare equal.
    return eq32(&fe.to_bytes(lhs), &fe.to_bytes(rhs));
}

/// Verify an ECDSA signature (r, s) on secp256k1 over a message hash, for a public key given as affine coordinates (Qx, Qy). Every argument
/// is big-endian, the ecosystem convention. Returns true exactly when the signature is valid by the SEC 1 verification equation. Composes
/// only the GREEN scalar field, the GREEN point ladder, and the GREEN group law — no new cryptography, no std.crypto, no secret.
pub fn verify(
    qx_be: *const [32]u8,
    qy_be: *const [32]u8,
    hash_be: *const [32]u8,
    r_be: *const [32]u8,
    s_be: *const [32]u8,
) bool {
    // invariant: the range gate runs before any arithmetic — r and s must lie in [1, n−1], or the signature is refused outright.
    const r_le = reverse32(r_be);
    const s_le = reverse32(s_be);
    if (!sc.is_canonical(&r_le) or !sc.is_canonical(&s_le)) return false; // r, s < n
    if (is_zero32(&r_le) or is_zero32(&s_le)) return false; // r, s ≥ 1

    // The public key must be a real point on the curve — an off-curve key is refused, not trusted.
    if (!on_curve(qx_be, qy_be)) return false;

    // Hash to a scalar mod n; the reference refuses a zero hash-scalar, and so does this file.
    const z_le = hash_to_scalar(hash_be);
    if (is_zero32(&z_le)) return false;

    // w = s⁻¹ mod n, then u1 = z·w and u2 = r·w — the two scalars the verification point is a combination of.
    const w = sc.invert(&s_le);
    const u1_le = sc.mul(&z_le, &w);
    const u2_le = sc.mul(&r_le, &w);

    // R = u1·G + u2·Q over the GREEN ladder and complete group law.
    const q = pt.Point{ .x = fe.from_bytes(qx_be), .y = fe.from_bytes(qy_be), .z = fe_one() };
    const r_pt = pt.add(mul_pt.scalarmul(base_point(), &u1_le), mul_pt.scalarmul(q, &u2_le));

    // A verification point at infinity has no affine x — such a signature is refused before the affine read (which would invert Z = 0).
    const z_bytes = fe.to_bytes(r_pt.z);
    if (is_zero32(&z_bytes)) return false;

    // invariant: R is finite here, so affine_x's single inversion is well-defined.
    const rx_be = pt.affine_x(r_pt);
    const vr_le = hash_to_scalar(&rx_be); // affine x reduced mod n, in the scalar field's order

    // Accept exactly when the reduced affine x equals r — the SEC 1 verification check.
    return eq32(&vr_le, &r_le);
}

// ---- selftest ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Independent reference: Zig's own std.crypto ECDSA over secp256k1 with SHA-256, used ONLY inside the selftest as a second implementation.
/// The primitive above never calls it.
const ZEcdsa = std.crypto.sign.ecdsa.EcdsaSecp256k1Sha256;
const Sha256 = std.crypto.hash.sha2.Sha256;

/// n − s (the malleable counterpart of s), computed by the independent reference's own scalar negation so no order-n limb pattern is pasted
/// into this file. Used only to prove the classic ECDSA malleability fact inside the selftest; big-endian in, big-endian out.
fn negate_scalar_be(s_be: *const [32]u8) [32]u8 {
    return std.crypto.ecc.Secp256k1.scalar.neg(s_be.*, .big) catch s_be.*;
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    // 0. On-curve gate: the base point is on the curve; a point one off in x is not.
    {
        if (!on_curve(&g_x_be, &g_y_be)) return error.BasePointOffCurve;
        var bad_x = g_x_be;
        bad_x[31] ^= 1; // shift the x-coordinate by one — almost surely off the curve
        if (on_curve(&bad_x, &g_y_be)) return error.OffCurveAccepted;
    }

    // 1. Independent-implementation parity plus algebraic tamper checks across a spread of deterministic key pairs and messages.
    {
        const messages = [_][]const u8{
            "",
            "a",
            "secp256k1 ecdsa parity",
            "The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog",
            "Grain — cryptography written in the open, checked against the world",
        };

        var seed_byte: u8 = 1;
        while (seed_byte <= 8) : (seed_byte += 1) {
            const seed = [_]u8{seed_byte} ** 32;
            const kp = try ZEcdsa.KeyPair.generateDeterministic(seed);
            const aff = kp.public_key.p.affineCoordinates();
            const qx = aff.x.toBytes(.big);
            const qy = aff.y.toBytes(.big);

            for (messages) |msg| {
                const sig = try kp.sign(msg, null); // deterministic signature
                // The reference agrees this signature is valid — the ground truth we check against.
                sig.verify(msg, kp.public_key) catch return error.ReferenceRejectedOwnSignature;

                const sb = sig.toBytes(); // r || s, big-endian
                var r_be: [32]u8 = undefined;
                var s_be: [32]u8 = undefined;
                var j: u32 = 0;
                while (j < 32) : (j += 1) {
                    r_be[j] = sb[j];
                    s_be[j] = sb[32 + j];
                }

                var h: [32]u8 = undefined;
                Sha256.hash(msg, &h, .{});

                // Our verifier accepts exactly what the reference accepts.
                if (!verify(&qx, &qy, &h, &r_be, &s_be)) return error.ValidSignatureRejected;

                // A one-bit tamper of r, s, or the hash is refused.
                {
                    var tr = r_be;
                    tr[0] ^= 1;
                    if (verify(&qx, &qy, &h, &tr, &s_be)) return error.TamperedRAccepted;
                    var ts = s_be;
                    ts[0] ^= 1;
                    if (verify(&qx, &qy, &h, &r_be, &ts)) return error.TamperedSAccepted;
                    var th = h;
                    th[0] ^= 1;
                    if (verify(&qx, &qy, &th, &r_be, &s_be)) return error.TamperedHashAccepted;
                    var tq = qx;
                    tq[31] ^= 1; // off-curve key
                    if (verify(&tq, &qy, &h, &r_be, &s_be)) return error.TamperedKeyAccepted;
                }

                // Out-of-range r or s (all zero) is refused.
                {
                    const zero = [_]u8{0} ** 32;
                    if (verify(&qx, &qy, &h, &zero, &s_be)) return error.ZeroRAccepted;
                    if (verify(&qx, &qy, &h, &r_be, &zero)) return error.ZeroSAccepted;
                }

                // Malleability fact: (r, n−s) verifies exactly when (r, s) does — the counterpart signature is equally valid.
                {
                    const s2 = negate_scalar_be(&s_be);
                    if (!verify(&qx, &qy, &h, &r_be, &s2)) return error.MalleableCounterpartRejected;
                }
            }
        }
    }

    print("GREEN crypto-secp256k1-ecdsa: ECDSA signature VERIFICATION on secp256k1 authored in pure Rye — Season G's composition over the " ++
        "GREEN scalar field (crypto/secp256k1_scalar.rye), point ladder (crypto/secp256k1_scalarmul.rye), group law " ++
        "(crypto/secp256k1_group.rye), and base field (crypto/fe_secp256k1.rye). Given a public key Q = (Qx, Qy), a message hash z, and a " ++
        "signature (r, s), verify refuses unless r and s lie in [1, n−1], folds the hash to a scalar mod n (refusing a zero hash-scalar as " ++
        "the reference does), computes w = s⁻¹ mod n and the two scalars u1 = z·w and u2 = r·w, forms R = u1·G + u2·Q, refuses the point at " ++
        "infinity, and accepts exactly when the affine x of R reduced mod n equals r. The base point G is the SEC 2 generator, entered as its " ++
        "published big-endian coordinate bytes and parsed through the proven base field, never raw limbs, with Z = 1. One honest " ++
        "strengthening beyond the reference's verify path: an ON-CURVE check (y² = x³ + 7) refuses an off-curve public key rather than " ++
        "trusting it. Byte order is the only product with the outside world — the ecosystem is big-endian, the scalar field and ladder walk " ++
        "little-endian, reversed at this file's boundary. The primitive calls no std.crypto. Parity holds against algebraic known-answers " ++
        "(a valid signature verifies; a one-bit tamper of r, s, the hash, or a coordinate is refused; an all-zero r or s is refused; an " ++
        "off-curve key is refused; and (r, n−s) verifies exactly when (r, s) does), and our authored Rye equals Zig's independent " ++
        "std.crypto.sign.ecdsa.EcdsaSecp256k1Sha256 true-for-true and false-for-false across a spread of deterministic key pairs and " ++
        "messages. secp256k1 is not in Monocypher, so the two witnesses are the algebraic answers and Zig's std.crypto. Purely local — in " ++
        "ECDSA VERIFICATION r, s, z, Q, and the inverse are all PUBLIC, so the variable-time arithmetic leaks no secret; ECDSA SIGNING with a " ++
        "real private key stays the custody gate (#3/#4), and a constant-time signing arithmetic stays a named horizon. No real key, no " ++
        "signing, no network, no funds, no real device. The secp256k1 tower now VERIFIES a signature — Kumara identity against a " ++
        "Bitcoin/Ethereum key, a Dimeroll receipt, a Comlink peer's claim — the reach the whole address arc was climbing toward.\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init_args: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init_args;
    return run_selftest();
}
